  {"a": "Willow", "p": "Aspirin was developed from a substance in the bark of which tree?", "s": "medicines and pharmacies", "w": ["Oak", "Chestnut", "Pine"]},
  {"a": "Cinchona bark", "e": "It came from South America, and Europe knew it as Jesuit's bark.", "p": "Which bark gave the world its first real treatment for malaria?", "s": "medicines and pharmacies", "w": ["Willow bark", "Birch bark", "Oak bark"]},
  {"a": "Quinine", "e": "Tonic water began as an easy way to take quinine in hot countries.", "p": "Which bitter medicine gives tonic water its taste?", "s": "medicines and pharmacies", "w": ["Aspirin", "Camphor", "Menthol"]},
  {"a": "The foxglove", "p": "Which garden flower gave doctors an early medicine for a weak heart?", "s": "medicines and pharmacies", "w": ["The sunflower", "The snowdrop", "The cornflower"]},
  {"a": "To hide a bitter taste", "p": "Why were pills once given a coating of sugar?", "s": "medicines and pharmacies", "w": ["To stop them breaking", "To keep them dry", "To make counting easy"]},
  {"a": "A pharmacy soda fountain", "e": "It was sold as a tonic, five cents a glass, in a pharmacy in Atlanta.", "p": "Coca-Cola was first sold in 1886 at what kind of counter?", "s": "medicines and pharmacies", "w": ["A railway station kiosk", "A hotel dining room", "A village grocery shop"]},
  {"a": "Grinding things to powder", "p": "What was a mortar and pestle used for in an old pharmacy?", "s": "medicines and pharmacies", "w": ["Measuring out exact drops", "Warming thick syrups", "Sealing glass bottles"]},
  {"a": "The drill", "e": "The dentist pumped with one foot, like a treadle sewing machine.", "p": "In the 1870s, what did a dentist's new foot pedal drive?", "s": "dentistry", "w": ["The lamp", "The chair", "The rinse pump"]},
  {"a": "The 1930s", "p": "In which decade did nylon bristles first appear on toothbrushes?", "s": "dentistry", "w": ["The 1890s", "The 1960s", "The 1980s"]},
  {"a": "Fluoride", "e": "Iodine was added to salt instead, to protect the thyroid gland.", "p": "Which mineral was added to water and toothpaste to protect teeth?", "s": "dentistry", "w": ["Magnesium", "Iodine", "Iron"]},
  {"a": "As a powder", "p": "Before the tube was invented, how was tooth cleaner usually sold?", "s": "dentistry", "w": ["As a syrup", "As a tablet", "As a spray"]},
  {"a": "Silk thread", "e": "A dentist in New Orleans suggested waxed silk in 1815.", "p": "What was the first dental floss made from?", "s": "dentistry", "w": ["Cotton string", "Horse hair", "Thin wire"]},
  {"a": "Elizabeth I", "e": "Sugar was costly, so dark teeth were once a sign of wealth.", "p": "Which English queen was said to have teeth blackened by sugar?", "s": "dentistry", "w": ["Victoria", "Anne", "Mary Tudor"]},
  {"a": "Pierre Fauchard", "e": "His book of 1728 described fillings, braces and false teeth.", "p": "Who is often called the father of modern dentistry?", "s": "dentistry", "w": ["Ambroise Paré", "Claude Bernard", "Louis Pasteur"]},
  {"a": "Cataract", "e": "The operation was called couching, and it is described in ancient Indian writing.", "p": "Ancient surgeons treated which eye trouble by pushing the cloudy lens aside?", "s": "sight and hearing", "w": ["Short sight", "Squint", "Dry eye"]},
  {"a": "The telephone", "e": "A carbon microphone from the telephone made the sound louder in the ear.", "p": "The first electric hearing aids used parts borrowed from which invention?", "s": "sight and hearing", "w": ["The gramophone", "The typewriter", "The camera"]},
  {"a": "Lentil", "p": "The word lens comes from the Latin name of which food?", "s": "sight and hearing", "w": ["Almond", "Olive", "Bean"]},
  {"a": "To widen the pupil", "e": "A wide pupil gives the doctor a clear view of the back of the eye.", "p": "Why does an eye doctor sometimes put drops in the eye before a test?", "s": "sight and hearing", "w": ["To wash out dust", "To cool the eye", "To stop blinking"]},
  {"a": "How clearly you see", "p": "What does a wall chart of letters measure at the eye doctor?", "s": "sight and hearing", "w": ["How well you see colour", "How wide your view is", "How fast your eyes move"]},
  {"a": "Glass", "e": "They were blown from glass and covered the white of the eye as well.", "p": "What were the first contact lenses, in the 1880s, made from?", "s": "sight and hearing", "w": ["Rubber", "Horn", "Plastic"]},
  {"a": "Hear a little better", "p": "What did an ear trumpet help a person to do?", "s": "sight and hearing", "w": ["Speak more loudly", "Breathe more easily", "See more sharply"]},
  {"a": "Mary Seacole", "e": "She paid her own way and ran a lodging house near the front.", "p": "Which nurse travelled from Jamaica to care for men in the Crimean War?", "s": "hospitals and nurses", "w": ["Clara Barton", "Edith Cavell", "Dorothea Dix"]},
  {"a": "Checking how shoes fit", "e": "Children loved watching their toes wiggle, until the danger was understood.", "p": "What were X-ray machines once used for in shoe shops?", "s": "hospitals and nurses", "w": ["Drying out wet leather", "Marking a price on soles", "Weighing a customer's foot"]},
  {"a": "Took in travellers", "e": "It shares its root with the word hospitality.", "p": "The word hospital first meant a place that did what?", "s": "hospitals and nurses", "w": ["Trained young doctors", "Kept medicines safe", "Housed old soldiers"]},
  {"a": "Sorting patients by need", "p": "In a hospital, what does the word triage mean?", "s": "hospitals and nurses", "w": ["Moving patients to a ward", "Recording a patient's past", "Washing before an operation"]},
  {"a": "About twenty minutes", "e": "It was also about a foot long, so it was awkward to carry.", "p": "Before 1866, how long did a fever thermometer take to give a reading?", "s": "hospitals and nurses", "w": ["About five seconds", "About one minute", "About two hours"]},
  {"a": "Helped them breathe", "p": "What did an iron lung do for a patient with polio?", "s": "hospitals and nurses", "w": ["Kept their legs straight", "Fed them slowly", "Kept them warm"]},
  {"a": "To show they were clean", "p": "Why did doctors begin to wear white coats in the late 1800s?", "s": "hospitals and nurses", "w": ["To keep themselves warm", "To hide their old clothes", "To match the hospital walls"]},
  {"a": "It cut itself off", "e": "The villagers agreed to stay inside their own parish for more than a year.", "p": "In 1665, the village of Eyam did what to stop the plague spreading?", "s": "epidemics", "w": ["It burned its fields", "It boiled all its water", "It sent its sick away"]},
  {"a": "A cook", "e": "She was never ill herself, which made her very hard to believe.", "p": "Mary Mallon spread typhoid in New York while working in which job?", "s": "epidemics", "w": ["A nurse", "A laundress", "A teacher"]},
  {"a": "Spain reported it openly", "e": "Other countries kept the news quiet in wartime, so Spain took the blame.", "p": "Why was the influenza of 1918 called Spanish flu?", "s": "epidemics", "w": ["It began in a Spanish town", "It spread fastest in Spain", "A Spanish doctor named it"]},
  {"a": "To clean mountain air", "e": "These mountain rest homes were called sanatoriums.", "p": "Before antibiotics, where were tuberculosis patients often sent?", "s": "epidemics", "w": ["To warm sea water", "To dry desert sand", "To deep forest caves"]},
  {"a": "The 1300s", "p": "In which century did the Black Death sweep across Europe?", "s": "epidemics", "w": ["The 1100s", "The 1500s", "The 1700s"]},
  {"a": "The mosquito", "e": "Ronald Ross proved it in 1897, by studying birds.", "p": "Which insect was found to carry the germ that causes malaria?", "s": "epidemics", "w": ["The flea", "The louse", "The tick"]},
  {"a": "They packed it with herbs", "e": "Sweet herbs were thought to keep out the bad air that carried plague.", "p": "Why did plague doctors wear a mask with a long beak?", "s": "epidemics", "w": ["They wanted to look fierce", "They spoke through the tip", "They kept tools inside it"]},
  {"a": "A new sewer system", "e": "The smell of the Thames drove parliament out of its own rooms.", "p": "The Great Stink of 1858 pushed London to build what?", "s": "clean water and sewers", "w": ["A clean water canal", "A public bath house", "A wider river bridge"]},
  {"a": "An aqueduct", "e": "Some ran for many miles on a very gentle downhill slope.", "p": "What did Roman engineers build to bring clean water into a city?", "s": "clean water and sewers", "w": ["A canal lock", "A stone pier", "A windmill"]},
  {"a": "Wash their clothes", "e": "The laundry room had hot water and drying racks that no small home had.", "p": "Besides bathing, what could people do at a Victorian public bath house?", "s": "clean water and sewers", "w": ["Buy their medicines", "Post their letters", "Store their coal"]},
  {"a": "At the Great Exhibition", "e": "It cost a penny, and more than 800,000 people paid it.", "p": "Where did many Londoners first try a public flushing toilet, in 1851?", "s": "clean water and sewers", "w": ["At a railway station", "At a city hospital", "In a public park"]},
  {"a": "Chlorine", "p": "Which chemical was added to city drinking water to kill germs?", "s": "clean water and sewers", "w": ["Ammonia", "Iodine", "Alcohol"]},
